\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Token-position entropy}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{entropy}

\p{Restrict to the token-generation specialization #{\mathcal A_q=\mathcal V}.
For #{t\geq1}, let #{\xi_t=A_{t-1}\in\mathcal V} be the token emitted at
position #{t}, and put #{h_t^{\mathrm{tok}}=\xi_{<t}}. Conditional on the run
reaching that position, define
#{\bar p_t(a)=\Pr(\xi_t=a\mid T\geq t)} under the declared task, policy, and
decoder. The
\strong{token-position entropy} is}

##{
  \mathcal H_t^{\mathrm{pos}}=-\sum_{a\in\mathcal V}\bar p_t(a)\log \bar p_t(a).
}

\p{This applies the entropy of \citet{Pt. I, Sec. 6}{shannon1948mathematical}
to a position-marginal law. It differs from the expected local entropy
#{\mathbb E[H(\pi_\theta(\cdot\mid h_t^{\mathrm{tok}}))]}; mixing token
prefixes can increase the marginal entropy.}

\p{The distinction matters when a paper reports high-entropy token positions
or forks. A position can be diverse because different histories choose
different deterministic tokens, even when each local policy is sharp.}
